      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 689,
      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and the planet’s climate. Here’s a concise breakdown of what makes water special:\n\n| Aspect | What it means |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Chemical identity** | Two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. Formula: **H₂O**. |\n| **Molecular shape** | Bent (V‑shaped) with a bond angle of ~104.5°. This gives water a **dipole moment**—one side is slightly negative (oxygen) and the other slightly positive (hydrogen). |\n| **Polarity & hydrogen bonding** | The polarity allows water molecules to form **hydrogen bonds** with each other and with many other substances. This gives water many of its unique properties. |\n| **States of matter** | Exists as a solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (vapor) under Earth’s normal temperature and pressure. The transitions are governed by temperature and pressure. |\n| **Key physical properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs a lot of heat before its temperature rises. <br>• **High surface tension** – allows insects to walk on it and creates droplets. <br>• **Excellent solvent** – dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “universal solvent.” <br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in winter. |\n| **Biological importance** | • **Cellular medium** – cells are ~70 % water. <br>• **Transport** – carries nutrients, gases, and waste. <br>• **Chemical reactions** – many metabolic reactions occur in aqueous environments. |\n| **Environmental role** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff. <br>• **Climate regulation** – water vapor is a powerful greenhouse gas; oceans store and redistribute heat. |\n| **Human uses** | • **Drinking & sanitation** – essential for health. <br>• **Industry** – cooling, cleaning, chemical production. <br>• **Agriculture** – irrigation. <br>• **Energy** – hydroelectric power, steam turbines. |\n\n### Why water is so special\n\n1. **Hydrogen bonding** gives water a high boiling point relative to its molecular weight, allowing it to remain liquid over a wide temperature range.  \n2. **Polarity** lets it dissolve salts, sugars, gases, and many organic molecules, making it indispensable for biochemical processes.  \n3. **Density anomaly** (ice floats) protects aquatic ecosystems during cold seasons.  \n4. **High heat capacity** buffers temperature swings in organisms and environments.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule that, through its polarity and hydrogen‑bonding ability, becomes a versatile solvent, a regulator of climate, and the medium in which life unfolds."
